WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.001
Deployment scripts often choose container
images, image tags, digests,

2
00:00:07.081 --> 00:00:13.163
registry locations, ConfigMaps, Secrets,
environment variables,

3
00:00:13.243 --> 00:00:15.235
and runtime settings.

4
00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:22.026
These inputs determine what code runs and
what data the workload can access.

5
00:00:22.106 --> 00:00:29.551
Treating them as ordinary parameters can
hide the fact that they shape both security

6
00:00:29.631 --> 00:00:32.776
posture and operational behavior.

7
00:00:32.856 --> 00:00:36.841
Image references deserve careful handling.

8
00:00:36.921 --> 00:00:43.946
Mutable tags can make it harder to know
exactly what image version was deployed.

9
00:00:44.026 --> 00:00:51.786
Digests or controlled release references
can improve traceability where the workflow

10
00:00:51.866 --> 00:00:53.229
supports them.

11
00:00:53.309 --> 00:00:55.930
Registry trust also matters.

12
00:00:56.010 --> 00:01:03.665
The script should use approved registries
and release channels rather than accepting

13
00:01:03.745 --> 00:01:08.358
arbitrary image sources or unreviewed
references.

14
00:01:08.438 --> 00:01:15.254
Configuration and secrets should be
environment-specific and reviewable.

15
00:01:15.334 --> 00:01:20.367
Secrets should not be placed in plaintext
scripts, logs,

16
00:01:20.447 --> 00:01:25.480
generated manifests, artifacts, or exposed
job output.

17
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:32.900
ConfigMaps and Secrets can contain
sensitive or operationally important values,

18
00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:40.425
so changes to them should be reviewed with
the same seriousness as workload changes.

19
00:01:40.505 --> 00:01:47.531
A deployment script should not turn secret
handling into a side effect of string

20
00:01:47.611 --> 00:01:48.974
substitution.

21
00:01:49.054 --> 00:01:56.289
Supply-chain checks such as provenance,
scanning, signing, approval metadata,

22
00:01:56.369 --> 00:02:02.975
or promotion gates can be integrated at a
defensive governance level where

23
00:02:03.055 --> 00:02:04.103
available.

24
00:02:04.183 --> 00:02:10.265
The goal is not to overload every script
with every possible control.

25
00:02:10.345 --> 00:02:17.685
The goal is to ensure the deployment
script respects the controls the organization

26
00:02:17.765 --> 00:02:20.911
relies on: approved image sources,

27
00:02:20.991 --> 00:02:27.387
traceable release references,
environment-specific configuration,

28
00:02:27.467 --> 00:02:32.500
and secret handling that does not leak
sensitive values.
